 ## Proof
 
 **Given:** The data and supplied access in the Statement.
 
-1.1 Let $U=\exp(iH/T)$. Its signed eigenphase is $\phi=\lambda/(2\pi T)$, with $|\phi|<1/(4\pi)$ because $|\lambda|\le K$ and $T=2K+1$. Thus the principal representative is injective on the promised spectrum. Choose a certified rational $\pi_+\in(\pi,4)$ and let $M$ be the least dyadic integer at least $128\pi_+T$, so $128\pi T\le M<256\pi_+T$. Choose an odd $r\ge8\ln(1/\nu)+8$ using a certified rational logarithm upper enclosure of additive width at most one, with the least such odd choice giving $r=O(1+\ln(1/\nu))$. Doubling and integer comparisons give finite schedules as in [F3]. [given, F3, construct, algebra]
+1.1 Let $U=\exp(iH/T)$. Its signed eigenphase is $\phi=\lambda/(2\pi T)$, with $|\phi|<1/(4\pi)$ because $|\lambda|\le K$ and $T=2K+1$. Thus the principal representative is injective on the promised spectrum. By [F7], choose a certified rational $\pi_+\in(\pi,4)$ (for example $22/7$) and let $M$ be the least dyadic integer at least $128\pi_+T$, so $128\pi T\le M<256\pi_+T$. Choose an odd $r\ge8\ln(1/\nu)+8$ using a certified rational logarithm upper enclosure of additive width at most one, with the least such odd choice giving $r=O(1+\ln(1/\nu))$. Doubling and integer comparisons give finite schedules as in [F3]. [given, F3, F7, construct, algebra]
 
 1.2 Prepare $\Phi_d$ once. Apply $r$ QPE trials to its same first factor, with fresh phase registers; preserve all phase outputs and arithmetic history. In an orthonormal eigenbasis $u_j$, $\Phi_d=d^{-1/2}\sum_j u_j\otimes\overline{u_j}$. The second factor is untouched and supplies a uniform orthogonal index in analysis. Conditional on $j$, controlled evolutions preserve $u_j$ and each fresh phase register has the kernel of [F1]; their conditional joint law is its $r$-fold product. Thus the median estimates one eigenvalue repeatedly, rather than independently redrawing spectral eigenvalues. No eigenbasis knowledge is required; repeated eigenvalues use any orthonormal basis within their eigenspaces. [F1, step 1.1, construct, algebra]
 
-1.3 Set $\eta=1/(32\pi T)$. Then $M\eta\ge4$, so [F2] bounds one trial's circular error probability by $1/6<1/4$. On circular success its signed estimate has distance below $\eta$ from $\phi$ and lies in $(-1/2,1/2)$: indeed $|\phi|+\eta<1/(4\pi)+1/(32\pi)<1/2$. Hence it is exactly the principal representative, and decoding by $2\pi T$ gives error below $1/16$. This derives the trial bound for this actual $M$ directly, without substituting it into a least-$M$ theorem. [F2, step 1.1, algebra]
