 2.1 Conditional on an eigenindex, let $X_i$ indicate an accurate exact trial and $S=\sum_iX_i$. By steps 1.2–1.3 the indicators are independent and each has success probability at least $3/4$. Markov's inequality applied to $3^{-S}$ gives $\Pr(S\le r/2)\le3^{r/2}\mathbb E3^{-S}\le(\sqrt3/2)^r$. For the elementary exponential estimate, apply [F5] on $[1,8/7]$: some $c\in(1,8/7)$ satisfies $\ln(8/7)=L(8/7)-L(1)=1/(7c)>1/8$. Since $2/\sqrt3>8/7$, monotonicity of $\ln$ (also immediate from [F5] and the mean value theorem) gives $\ln(2/\sqrt3)>1/8$, so [F6] gives $(\sqrt3/2)^r\le\exp(-r/8)\le\nu\exp(-1)$. Applying [F5] on $[1,2]$ gives $\ln2=1/c_2<1$ for some $c_2\in(1,2)$, hence [F6] gives $\exp(-1)<1/2$. The conditional failure is therefore below $\nu/2$. An inaccurate odd median requires at least half the trials to be inaccurate. Therefore the exact decoded median has error at most $1/16$ with conditional failure below $\nu/2$. This is the explicit conditional median argument also used in [F3]. [F3, F5, F6, step 1.2, step 1.3, algebra]
 
